                        "form": "more terrene"
                    },
                    {
                        "form_id": "7d2e2e5c-9f73-4970-a6fd-34acbe370fa8",
                        "entry_id": "2f63485f-9d1a-324b-a056-a121ba3dc098",
                        "tags": [
                            "superlative"
                        ],
                        "form": "most terrene"
                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "0f28914e-a4f4-3e43-a563-f8d629e803c3",
                        "entry_id": "2f63485f-9d1a-324b-a056-a121ba3dc098",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Pertaining to earth or the material world; earthly, terrestrial (as opposed to heavenly or marine)."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "82fcd9bd-fe0f-415d-97d9-783f4be48090",
                                "sense_id": "0f28914e-a4f4-3e43-a563-f8d629e803c3",
                                "text": "God set before him a mortal and immortal life, a nature celestial and terrene."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "8898ee5e-2261-4652-9db9-c888d489bdf2",
                                "sense_id": "0f28914e-a4f4-3e43-a563-f8d629e803c3",
                                "text": "The common conceptions of the matters which lie at the basis of our terrene experience"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "7d5a3097-1d30-48fb-a676-eebc2d86d39a",
                                "sense_id": "0f28914e-a4f4-3e43-a563-f8d629e803c3",
                                "text": "One had never thought of the sea as the great place of safety, but now it came over one that there is no place so safe from the land. When it does not give you trouble it takes it away—takes away letters and telegrams and newspapers and visits and duties and efforts, all the complications, all the superfluities and superstitions that we have stuffed into our terrene life."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "bdc99ed8-8f01-4242-8b22-4219c3658334",
                                "sense_id": "0f28914e-a4f4-3e43-a563-f8d629e803c3",
                                "text": "Arius, warring his life long upon the consubstantiality of the Son with the Father, and Valentine, spurning Christ’s terrene body, and the subtle African heresiarch Sabellius who held that the Father was Himself His own Son."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "6a2add96-3215-4aaf-a9e0-48557d62b15e",
                                "sense_id": "0f28914e-a4f4-3e43-a563-f8d629e803c3",
                                "text": "For the earth was both celestial and terrene, the down here and the up there."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "9c13b76e-05de-3799-bae8-773b4da74408",
                        "entry_id": "2f63485f-9d1a-324b-a056-a121ba3dc098",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Made of matter (as opposed to antimatter)."
                        ],
